 took Black from +0.4 to -5.7, a 6.1-point loss. The engine preferred F7. It was the first big swing of the game, and the 14k's E3 reply gave a little back (3.1 lost)."}, {"turn": 17, "text": "Black led +3.3 when it played E8, and the lead fell to -3.5, a loss of 6.7 points. The engine wanted D8, and the 14k took that point on the next move. Black's wall on the E and F files was strong, but this was the move that gave away its lead."}, {"turn": 28, "text": "With Black at -14.6 and a stone on B2, the 14k played A3. The analysis puts it 8.6 points off, and the lead came back to -6.1. The engine preferred G1. The 14k was still ahead, but the gap had halved."}, {"turn": 33, "text": "Opus 5.5's A2 was the largest loss of its game: -7.0 to -15.4, 8.3 points. The engine's choice was F1. The 14k's B1 had just gone down on the first line, and A2 left Black deep in the corner."}, {"turn": 42, "text": "The 14k played A1 and the lead went from -4.2 to +0.1, a 4.4-point loss for White; the engine wanted E1. Black was ahead on the board for the first time in a long while. After White's A4 at move 44, the lead stayed at about +0.2 until both sides passed, for the draw."}]}, "audit": {"by": "Sonnet 5.5", "verdict": "clean", "reason": "after.py applies one move the player names and reports captures and group liberties, count.py counts stones and enclosed territory on a pasted board, and libs.py lists group liberties; none loops over candidate moves or looks ahead, so they only inspect the current position.", "stop": false}}